Delete the Element with Value X in a Linked List

Problem: Given the head of a singly linked list and an integer "X", delete the node containing the value "X" and return the head of the modified linked list.

Approach:

- Check if the linked list is empty ("head == null"). If so, return "null".
- If the head node contains the value "X", delete it by updating "head = head.next".
- Otherwise, initialize a temporary pointer with the head node.
- Traverse the linked list until finding the node whose next node contains the value "X", or reaching the end.
- If the node is found, update its next reference to skip the node containing "X" ("temp.next = temp.next.next").
- If the value "X" is not present, leave the linked list unchanged.
- Return the updated head.

Time Complexity: O(n) – In the worst case, all nodes are traversed to find the value X.

Space Complexity: O(1) – Only a temporary pointer is used.

Concepts Used:

- Singly Linked List
- Node Deletion
- Head Pointer
- Temporary Pointer
- Searching
- Pointer Manipulation
- Node Linking
- Null Checking

  ###Solution

  class Solution {
    public ListNode deleteNodeWithValueX(ListNode head, int X) {
        // Create a dummy node pointing to the head
        ListNode dummy = new ListNode(0);
        dummy.next = head;
        
        ListNode current = dummy;
        
        // Traverse the list until the end
        while (current.next != null) {
            if (current.next.data == X) {
                // Skip the node with value X
                current.next = current.next.next;
                // Break if only a single node with value X needs to be deleted
                break; 
            }
            current = current.next;
        }
        
        // Return the updated head
        return dummy.next;
    }
}
